WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.060
Speaker 1: Let's now speak to some of those
very folks in our audience.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.509
We do this on the Friday show.

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:06.730
We do it via discord.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:13.000
You can find the discord at David Pacman dot
com slash discord.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.700
That is the place to do it.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.699
We are going to start today with john from
Connecticut.

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:21.510
John from connecticut, welcome to the program.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:22.590
What's on your mind today?

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.910
Speaker 2: Hey, can you hear me?

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:26.910
Yes.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:27.910
Cool.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:34.510
So my question is more along the lines of
why does it seem like so many people nowadays,

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:40.840
especially on the right, seem to have a problem
with any critical thinking, Like it's so hard

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.820
for people to understand, to like, you know,
look at a situation that they're not used

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:49.440
to understanding and they can't like, they
just immediately jump to conclusions.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.079
Speaker 1: Two answers to that.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:57.940
Number one, there are some people who just
have not been taught how to think critically,

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:58.940
and so they don't.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:00:59.940
Right.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.089
And in a sense, you can't blame them to a
degree because if you haven't been taught

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:07.189
something, how can you be expected to know
how to do it?

00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:13.180
On the other hand, there are those on the
right who recognize that when people really

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:20.530
apply basic critical thinking and media literacy
techniques to right wing ideology, it very

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.119
quickly falls apart.

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:27.119
And this is why those who are wise to this
actually don't want critical thinking taught

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:28.119
in schools.

00:01:28.119 --> 00:01:33.310
They realize if too many people start thinking
critically, it's bad for the Republican Party.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.930
So it's a combination of ignorance and bad
faith actors and in some cases, maybe a combination

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:39.930
of the two.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.040
Speaker 2: What do you think is more prevalent
bad faith actors or people who are just ignorant?

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.899
Speaker 1: I think among voters, the average
voter, I think it's ignorance.

00:01:47.899 --> 00:01:51.560
And listen, the left is not exempt from this
by any means.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.619
There is a lack of critical thinking on left
and right, and we could slice and dice it.

00:01:55.619 --> 00:02:00.810
But I think that the vast majority of those
who aren't thinking critically aren't thinking

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:04.719
critically because of ignorance, not because
they are bad faith actors.

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:11.300
They've been sort of indoctrinated as a result
of not thinking for themselves in a critical

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.300
way.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.500
And it's really an indictment on the educational
system and the media system as well.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.980
Speaker 2: All right.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:23.030
So the last thing then is what do you think
would be the best way to, like, talk to the

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:28.379
people who are more ignorant, that are kind
of easier to, you know, maybe give them some

00:02:28.379 --> 00:02:31.720
evidence to hopefully change their mind on
that type of situation.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:37.690
Speaker 1: The best path for the folks you're
talking about is actually not to give them

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:38.700
evidence.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:44.470
It's to know how to use a gentle, empathetic,
Socratic method of questioning to get them

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.190
to say, Hey, maybe I'm not actually that solid
in what I believe.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:50.190
So it's.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.480
Where did you learn that?

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.970
Oh, how do you know that's trustworthy in
general?

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:56.570
How do you know what things are?

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:57.570
Trustworthy?

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:00.770
What's a good way of figuring out what to
believe?

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:05.480
That approach, rather than beating people
over the head with evidence, is probably going

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.480
to be more effective.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.440
Speaker 2: All right.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:09.440
Thank you very much, David.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.440
All right.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.170
Speaker 1: John from Connecticut, great to
hear from you.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:17.380
Why don't we go next to Nicole from Utah?

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.980
Nicole, welcome to the program.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.980
Speaker 2: Thank you so much to David.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.849
My question this time is actually related
to what Trump has said in relation to the

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:27.110
Ukraine war.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:32.510
Do you think he has any chance if he does
get elected, which is a long shot anyways?

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:36.360
Does he have any amount of chance at stopping
this war?

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.510
He claims.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.380
Speaker 1: Listen, you will.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.629
He says, let's go through it in parts.

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:47.010
Trump says he will end the Russian invasion
of Ukraine in 24 hours.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.750
When he's asked how, he says, Oh, I'll talk
to them.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:50.750
Pretty silly.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.750
Pretty dumb.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:04:00.079
The only way that I see that conflict as ending
quickly would be if basically Ukraine gives

00:04:00.079 --> 00:04:02.500
up and Russia just takes over.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:07.360
So even in the best scenario, it's not going
to end in 24 hours.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.159
Now, does Trump have a chance at actually
ending it?

00:04:12.159 --> 00:04:17.800
I think he does have a chance in the sense
that every every president has a chance.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:22.410
The question is the detail of how you would
actually do it.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.720
But sure, I mean, Trump has a chance.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.250
He just doesn't have a great track record.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.460
That makes me think it's highly likely.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.750
Speaker 2: Mm hmm.

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:36.220
That's all I have for you today, David.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.220
All right.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:38.220
Speaker 1: Thank you, Nicole.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:05:51.569
Nicole from Utah, Great to hear from you once
again.

